  /* --- real-world figures for the car --- */
  vtop_kmh:205,          /* fastest it goes. A Supercar tops about 250 on the Esplanade.      */
  gAcc:0.80,             /* acceleration, g. A Supercar does 0-100 in about 3.5 s.            */
  gBrk:1.75,             /* braking, g. With this aero package, 1.5-1.8.                      */
  gLat:1.60,             /* cornering grip, g.                                                */
  carLen_m:4.98,         /* Camaro ZL1 length.                                                */
  vfloor_kmh:55,         /* it never crawls below this, real terms. The hairpin sits near it. */
  /* --- derived kerb cue: a low, single lift per lap. It is deliberately not claimed as an
         event-plan feature until K220–K231 placement is supplied. Gravity still does the fall. --- */
  jumpAir_s:0.15,
  jumpEvery_s:8.0,       /* secondary time guard; the lap gate is the actual cap                 */
  jumpTrig:0.88,
  jumpRoll:.08,
  /* v5.66 — SMOKE AND RUBBER. Andrew Fisher, 22 Sep 2026: on the day and night looks, smoke and rubber
     marks when drifting round corners.

     The smoke was already here and could not be seen. It was made sparse on purpose — one puff every tenth of
     a second, living one second, twenty-four of them, drawn ADDITIVELY at a sixth opacity and then halved
     again by day. Additive is the part that mattered: an additive puff can only ever make the road brighter,
     and tyre smoke on a grey road in daylight is a thing you see because it is PALER and SOFTER than what is
     behind it, not because it glows. So it is alpha-blended now, a little more of it, living a little longer.
     The original worry stands and the caps stay: a bounded count, a bounded life, and it only ever appears
     when the car is genuinely sliding. */
  /* v5.71 — AND IT WAS STILL TOO WEAK TO SEE. Andrew Fisher, 22 Sep 2026, looking at the built v5.66:
     the tyre marks and smoke were there but far too weak. He is right. The caution above was
     overdone: a sixth of an opacity, sixty puffs alive at a time and half of that again taken off by day
     comes to nothing you can actually see on a grey road. So it is dense now — three times the opacity,
     nearly three times as many, living half again as long and growing bigger as they go — and the caps are
     still caps, still bounded, and it still only appears where a tyre is genuinely turning against the road. */
  smokeEvery_s:.015, smokeLife_s:3.1, maxSmoke:300, maxSparks:0,
  smokeAlpha:.54, smokeGrow:2.05, smokeDimDay:.82, smokeDimNight:1.0,
  /* THE BURNOUT. Andrew Fisher, 22 Sep 2026: smoking tyres in the showcase, like a burnout,
     and when drifting in parts too.

     The car already sat on the line for three and a half seconds before it launched, doing nothing. Now it
     lights the rears up: they spin against a car that only creeps, both contact patches boil, rubber goes
     down underneath it, and at burnTo_s it hooks up and goes. burnSpin_ms is the wheel's speed while the car
     is doing about two — that difference IS the smoke, and it is why the rear wheels have their own angle.

     One number governs the lot. m.burn says how hard the rears are turning against the road, the burnout
     drives it on the grid and a slide drives it out on the circuit, and everything reads it: how much smoke,
     how big, how long it hangs, how dark the rubber. That is why a big slide now makes a cloud and a small
     one still makes a wisp — same number, same emitter, one road speed apart. */
