       that read as pale concrete from edge to edge: measured against his own frame, mean colour went from
       [66 89 105] to [99 106 104] and saturation from 0.38 to 0.10 — the picture had gone grey. These
       darken and cool the ground so the built things read against it again, and the water is lifted to the
       colour his frame actually renders ([56 90 122]/255, solved back through base*tint+lift rather than
       dialled by eye) — then pulled off the red, because solving for his colour in isolation is not the
       same as solving for it against this ground. Lifted evenly the water came out brighter than the darkened
       land in every channel and read as warmth rather than water: removing it took 44 of red away and only 34
       of blue. What matters is not the water's own colour on a swatch but the colour it puts INTO the picture
       against what is behind it, so the red lift comes down and the blue goes up and the canals read blue. Asked which he wanted on 18 Sep 2026, he said bring the first one back. */
    mat:{ground:{tint:[.42,.41,.395],lift:[0,0,.002]},street:{tint:[.50,.49,.475],lift:[0,0,.002]},deck:{tint:[.72,.72,.72],lift:[.025,.025,.028]},walls:{tint:[.08,.18,.34],lift:[.11,.11,.11]},kerb:{tint:[1.05,1.00,.96],lift:[.01,.01,.01]},barrier:{tint:[1.9,1.9,1.9],lift:[.10,.10,.10]},water:{tint:[2,2,2],lift:[.100,.262,.430]},beach:{tint:[1.15,1.10,.95],lift:[.055,.045,.025]},park:{tint:[.52,1.38,.60],lift:[.004,.032,.008]},tree:{tint:[1.6,4.2,1.7],lift:[.015,.14,.02]}},
    effects:{shoulder:false,roadLight:false,energyTrail:false,tailRibbons:false,barrierPulse:false,beam:false,beacons:false,motes:false,crowdFlashes:false,roadGlow:false,cityLights:false,treeGlow:false,surf:true}},
  showcase:{name:'showcase',label:'After Dark Showcase',day:0,sky:'transparent',bloom:.95,bloomCut:.30,
    /* v5.79 — THE NIGHT, BACK. Andrew Fisher, 23 Sep 2026: every track and every look — the quality needed
       improving everywhere. What had happened to it, measured on the same chase frame in the strip of the
       picture clear of the caption panel: the build he approved on 17 Sep (hosted-52) read lum .183 with 26% of
       the frame lit; v5.50, which recorded docs/look_baseline and put the sRGB 2.4 display curve on the night
       ("physical linear-light surfaces"), read .296 and 92% lit — the same scene, every dark value lifted; and
       from v5.64, when Car focus became the default and took the caption panel off the frame, the gate's whole
       frame went to .42 and 100% lit. The baseline's whole-frame figures had "held" only because the panel's
       dark left half was in them. Below is the night as approved, on this renderer: no display curve
       (nightGamma 1) and the .30 knee hosted-52 had (nightKnee), a 1.4 gain over its linear frame — and from the
       air a longer exposure (nightFar: ×2 by the time the camera is 200 units — 1.2 km — off its target,
       nothing at all under 60), because a night city from 1.7 km up is dark and a camera opens up for it; the
       route's lines brighten as they go thinner than a pixel (lines.thinFloor, see LINE_VS), which is what makes
       the lit circuit a ribbon through the city from the wide and overhead cameras rather than a thread; the
       facades come down to .62 with the windows lifted to .9, so a building is dark with its lights on rather than
       a beige box; and the route's core and glow ease back a touch (.55/.30) so the edge is a lit line, not a
       tube. Measured on the gate's own five dark shots (1400×345, Car focus on): chase lum .186 · 22% lit ·
       orange .032 · white .0021; heli .160 · 17% · .024; wide .144 · 16% · .0051; top .155 · 19% · .0043;
       onboard .179 · 19% · .029 — against hosted-52's .183 and 26% in its clear strip, and against the washed
       frame this replaces at .42 and 99%. The LED and Coates orange
