  S.look=G.LOOKS[o.look]||G.LOOKS[S.tune.look]||G.LOOKS.day;
  /* v5.49 — IT OPENS AT WHAT THE SCREEN CAN CARRY, NOT AT THE TOP OF THE LADDER. v5.48 opened at Ultra on the
     reasoning that the ladder would give detail back if the machine could not hold it. Andrew Fisher, 18 Sep
     2026: it should never lag — the one he supplied worked, and looked and ran better. He is right and
     the reasoning was wrong twice over. The ladder could not save it — it only looked every 60 frames and
     needed two readings, so a machine managing half a frame a second would have taken an hour to give up its
     first rung (fixed in the show adapter, and a frame over 400 ms now counts on its own). And even with a
     working ladder, opening too high means the first thing he sees is the bad version: it has to stutter
     before it is allowed to improve, and a first impression is not something you get to take back.

     So it opens where the picture is certain to move, and the pixel count is the thing that decides. Ultra
     draws 1.25x the pixels of High with a 3072 shadow map, and on a 4K panel at device-pixel-ratio 2 that is
     nine million pixels a frame. Above about 2.6 million it opens at High; above about 5 it opens at Balanced.
     A big desktop screen still gets Ultra, which is where his "high detail, track and camaro" lives. Nothing
