  const t=S.clock,st=SND.st,ta=at!=null?at:now();
  /* v5.57 — ONE FRAME, ONE TICK.
     Two places drive the sound on every frame: the show adapter's own loop and the camera frame, and both were
     calling this. The whole of the engine's scheduling — the rate, the gains, the filters, the intake, the
     scrub, the pan, the Doppler — was therefore written twice per frame, the second write landing on the first.
     That is double the Web Audio parameter traffic for nothing.

     The guard is on the SCENE CLOCK, not on wall time, so it is exactly "have I already sounded this frame?".
     The first caller of a frame does the work and the second returns; the offline render passes its own audio
     time and is never skipped, because there every call is its own moment. Nothing about what is scheduled, or
     when, changes — only how many times the same instruction is written. */
  if(t<st.lastT-.5)SND.sceneReset(ta);          /* the fallback: a clock that rewound without G.simReset (v5.42) */
  st.lastT=t;
