})(window.GC3D = window.GC3D || {});
/* GC500 — the geospatial showcase backdrop: Astra's runtime, mounted on the page's own terms.

   WHAT THIS IS. A second 3D backdrop beside the existing one, not a replacement for it. The existing scene
   (GC3D.mount) stays exactly as it is and stays the fallback, so a device that cannot run this one loses
   nothing it had. Choosing this backdrop mounts the supplied runtime — physically based surfaces, a real
   astronomical sun and moon, a dynamic shadow, bloom, and a TRON look built from feature edges — over the same
   flat circuit drawing that has always been underneath.

   THE RULES IT KEEPS, WHICH ARE THE PAGE'S RULES AND NOT THE RUNTIME'S.
     · One renderer and one GL context at a time. Mounting this unmounts the other, and the reverse.
     · Nothing is allocated until the Showcase is actually open. Today makes no request and builds no scene.
     · Hidden tab, closed showcase and Motion: Off all stop it, and none of them tears the scene down for a
       moment's absence — the same distinction the existing scene learned on 19 Sep 2026.
     · WebGL2 with EXT_color_buffer_float or it does not start, says why in one line, and the flat circuit
       stays. A lost context is the same case, not a crash.
     · No network. The tiles are built on the device and served to the runtime through its own embedded
       resolver, which refuses to fall back to a fetch. The file and shared editions therefore work from
       storage with nothing behind them, which is what they are for.

   WHAT IT IS NOT. Not a survey, not telemetry, and not a Coates asset position. The shapes are OpenStreetMap
   footprints on a registered schematic frame; the lap it flies is the runtime's own scene clock and not a
   recorded lap. Equipment positions live on the map, come from the delivery package's own registration, and
   never come from here.

