  /* WHEN THE SCENE THINKS IT IS.

     The sun and moon are real, computed for this site from a real instant. The lit look used to follow the
     actual clock, which is honest and was often what somebody wanted — but it meant "the lit circuit" was
     whatever hour you happened to open it at, so a day look opened after tea came back as night and the two
     looks were not reliably two different things.

     Andrew Fisher, 21 Sep 2026: night is night time and day is daytime on the Surfers track.
     So each look names its own hour and holds it. Nothing is faked — the almanac still does all the work,
     computing the real sun and moon for this site at that instant. It is simply asked about a fixed moment
     rather than about now. */
  const DAY_HOUR_UTC = 1;     /* 11:00 Brisbane — sun high and to the north, which is what a Surfers day is */
  const DAY_MIN_UTC = 0;
  const NIGHT_HOUR_UTC = 9;   /* 19:00 Brisbane — an hour and twenty after sunset in September: properly dark */
  const NIGHT_MIN_UTC = 0;
  function instantFor(look) {
    if (Q.at) return Q.at;                     /* set by hand only, to look at an hour that is not one of these */
